    "body": "Okay, this is a bit of a wild one.\r\n\r\nIt seems that when `sh` is invoked in `git-bash`, and has input piped to it, that input piping extends to all child processes as well.\r\n\r\nSo our script reads everything up to `install <blah blah>`, which causes it to call into code that has already been read (but not executed). While executing this code, `unzip` is forked (I guess CreateProcess-ed). At that point, the pipe is transferred from `sh` to `unzip`, and `unzip` reads *the remainder of the install script as input* (basically everything in the shell script that wasn't already read), which is resulting in these wacky inputs.\r\n\r\nI can solve this on Windows by reading input from `/dev/tty` (e.g. `unzip $file < /dev/tty`, but I don't know how well that's going to transfer across platforms, and perhaps more importantly, it's not clear to me that this is at all the correct behavior for a shell.",
